    "q-43005": {
      "dom_id": "step-q43005",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $Q$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${Q}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,4 & \\,1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Q$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,4 & \\,1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,-1 & \\,-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Q}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
