    "q-131385": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131385",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $U$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${U}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We write down $U$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $U$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${U}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{U}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
