    "q-131999": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131999",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
